.TH LAN\-SCAN 1 "2026-10-06" "mu2edaq-cluster-tools 1.0.1" "Mu2e DAQ Manual"
.SH NAME
lan\-scan \- probe the local LAN and list responding hosts
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B lan\-scan
.RB [ \-n
.IR CIDR ]
.RB [ \-c
.IR FILE ]
.RB [ \-t
.IR SECONDS ]
.RB [ \-w
.IR COUNT ]
.RB [ \-p
.IR LIST ]
.RB [ \-\-no\-dns ]
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.br
.B lan\-scan
.BR \-\-version | \-h
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B lan-scan
discovers hosts on the local network by sending an ICMP ping (and,
optionally, TCP connect probes) to every address in a subnet, then
prints a table of the hosts that responded along with their reverse-DNS
name and IP address. Progress
.RB ( "Scanning <cidr> (<n> addresses)..." )
and notes go to standard error; results go to standard output.
.PP
If no network is given, the local IPv4 network(s) are auto-detected from
the host's network interfaces (\fBip addr\fR on Linux, \fBifconfig\fR on
macOS/BSD, \fBipconfig\fR on Windows). Loopback networks are dropped and
the
.I first
remaining network is scanned; when there are several, the others are named
on standard error. On a multi-homed DAQ node that may not be the network you
meant \(em give
.B \-\-network
explicitly.
.SH PROBES AND SAFETY
For every host address in the network
.RB ( ipaddress.hosts() ,
i.e. excluding the network and broadcast addresses of anything larger than
a /31), one worker thread:
.IP 1. 4
runs the system
.BR ping (8)
once
.RB ( "ping \-c 1 \-W" " \fItimeout\fR\-in\-milliseconds" ;
.B ping \-n 1 \-w
on Windows) as a subprocess, killed after
.IR timeout +1
seconds;
.IP 2. 4
only if the ping failed and
.B \-\-ports
is set, attempts a full TCP connect to each listed port in turn, closing the
connection as soon as one succeeds;
.IP 3. 4
only for a responder, and unless
.BR \-\-no\-dns ,
does one reverse lookup with
.BR gethostbyaddr (3).
.PP
Up to
.B \-\-workers
(default 64) addresses are probed concurrently. There is no rate limit, no
inter-probe delay, no confirmation and no upper bound on the network size:
the whole address list is built in memory before the first probe, so a
mistyped
.B /16
runs 65534 ping processes, 64 at a time, and a
.B /8
about 16.7 million.
With the default timeout a silent address holds its worker for about 2 s on
Linux (see BUGS), so a /24 with few responders takes about 8 s and a /16
about 35 minutes. Nothing is sent other than the ICMP echo, the TCP SYN
(and handshake) and the DNS query; no payload is written to an open port.
.PP
Scanning address space you do not administer \(em including Fermilab
networks outside the DAQ enclave \(em may be treated as hostile by the site's
intrusion detection. The shipped
.I config/lan_scan\-mu2edaq.yaml
targets
.BR 131.225.245.0/24 ,
a routable Fermilab subnet.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-n\fR, \fB\-\-network\fR \fICIDR\fR
Network to scan, e.g. \fB192.168.1.0/24\fR. Default: auto-detect.
.TP
\fB\-c\fR, \fB\-\-config\fR \fIFILE\fR
YAML configuration file supplying defaults for any of the options below.
See \fIconfig/lan_scan.yaml.example\fR.
.TP
\fB\-t\fR, \fB\-\-timeout\fR \fISECONDS\fR
Per-host probe timeout. Default: 1.0. Applied to the ping, to each TCP
connect, and (nominally) to the DNS lookup. On Linux the effective ping
limit is
.IR SECONDS +1;
see
.BR BUGS .
.TP
\fB\-w\fR, \fB\-\-workers\fR \fICOUNT\fR
Number of concurrent probe threads. Default: 64.
.TP
\fB\-p\fR, \fB\-\-ports\fR \fILIST\fR
Comma-separated TCP ports to try (e.g. \fB22,80,443\fR) if a host does
not answer ICMP ping, such as one behind a firewall that filters it.
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-dns\fR
Skip reverse DNS lookups; report IP addresses only. Faster on large
subnets. There is no option to turn lookups back on when the configuration
file sets
.BR "no_dns: true" ,
and no environment variable for it.
.TP
\fB\-\-json\fR
Print results as a JSON array of
.B {"ip": ..., "hostname": ...}
objects instead of a table.
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Show help and exit.
.TP
\fB\-\-version\fR
Print the version number and exit. This prints
.BR 1.0.0 ,
from
.IR lan_scan.py ;
the installed package is 1.0.1. See
.BR BUGS .
.SH CONFIGURATION PRECEDENCE
For every option, the effective value is chosen in this order:
commandline flag, then environment variable, then the \fB\-\-config\fR
YAML file, then the built-in default. The configuration file is read only
when
.B \-\-config
is given; there is no default path and no environment variable for it.
Recognised keys are
.BR network ,
.BR timeout ,
.BR workers ,
.B ports
(list or comma-separated string) and
.BR no_dns .
.SH FILES
.TP
.I config/lan_scan\-mu2edaq.yaml
Shipped scan profile:
.BR 131.225.245.0/24 ,
ports 22, 80, 443.
.TP
.I config/lan_scan\-mu2edaq\-data.yaml
Shipped scan profile:
.BR 10.226.9.0/24 ,
ports 22, 80, 443.
.TP
.I config/lan_scan\-mu2edaq\-ipmi.yaml
Shipped scan profile:
.BR 192.168.246.0/24 ,
ports 22, 80, 443.
.TP
.I config/lan_scan.yaml.example
Annotated template for a new scan profile.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B LAN_SCAN_NETWORK
Overrides the network to scan (same syntax as \fB\-\-network\fR).
.TP
.B LAN_SCAN_TIMEOUT
Overrides the per-host probe timeout in seconds.
.TP
.B LAN_SCAN_WORKERS
Overrides the number of concurrent probe threads.
.TP
.B LAN_SCAN_PORTS
Overrides the TCP fallback port list (same syntax as \fB\-\-ports\fR).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Scan completed (regardless of how many hosts responded).
.TP
.B 1
Could not auto-detect a local network and none was given.
.TP
.B 2
The network given via \fB\-\-network\fR, an environment variable, or the
config file was not a valid CIDR; or a command-line usage error.
.PP
A missing or unreadable
.B \-\-config
file, a non-numeric timeout or worker count from the environment or the
config file, a non-numeric entry in a port list from any source, or a worker
count below 1, ends in a Python traceback with status 1.
A non-numeric
.B \-t
or
.B \-w
on the command line is an argparse error, status 2.
.SH EXAMPLES
Scan the auto-detected local subnet:
.PP
.RS
.B lan\-scan
.RE
.PP
Scan a specific subnet, skipping DNS lookups:
.PP
.RS
.B lan\-scan \-\-network 10.0.0.0/24 \-\-no\-dns
.RE
.PP
Also try common TCP ports for hosts that filter ICMP, and emit JSON:
.PP
.RS
.B lan\-scan \-\-ports 22,80,443 \-\-json
.RE
.PP
Use a shipped profile, with a gentler concurrency:
.PP
.RS
.B lan\-scan \-c config/lan_scan\-mu2edaq\-data.yaml \-w 16
.RE
.SH BUGS
On Linux,
.B ping \-W
takes seconds (iputils), but the value passed is milliseconds, so
.B \-W 1000
asks for a 1000 s wait; the subprocess timeout of
.IR timeout +1
seconds is what actually ends the probe. macOS
.B ping \-W
takes milliseconds and is correct.
.PP
The DNS timeout is applied with
.BR socket.setdefaulttimeout ,
which does not bound
.BR gethostbyaddr (3)
and is process-global across the worker threads.
.PP
.B \-\-version
reports 1.0.0 while the package metadata is 1.0.1.
.PP
Importing the
.B lan_scan
module runs the package's
.IR __init__.py ,
which imports
.B ssh_selector
and therefore Textual, although
.B lan\-scan
does not use it.
.PP
See
.I docs/ISSUES\-PENDING.md
in the source tree.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ssh\-selector (1),
.BR ping (8)
